 raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3.$ So we have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}=\\frac{8}{125}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n10{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=10\\cdot \\frac{8}{125}=\\frac{80}{125}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{80}{125}=\\frac{16\\cdot 5}{25\\cdot 5}=\\frac{16}{25}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544797 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544797 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68718" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544797 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544797 #edb/ref 1071 "${(\\frac{3}{7})}^{3}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544797 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2545d131-b88a-533a-a3f0-2d6b5ef7d401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544797 #edb/ref 1074 "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{7})}^{3}=\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{7}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${7}^{3}=343,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{7}^{3}}=\\frac{27}{343}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544861 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544861 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544861 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544861 #edb/ref 1071 "${(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544861 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a48af0b4-b2cb-5194-ba80-dd04350d6f6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544861 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $-\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{(-2)}{3}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $4$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $4:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}={(\\frac{(-2)}{3})}^{4}=\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{{3}^{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-2)^{4}=16$ and ${3}^{4}=81,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{{3}^{4}}=\\frac{16}{81}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544900 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544966 #edb/ref 175921860